  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 1020 PM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nWe are still on track for warm and dry weather through the end of \nthe week as the axis of the upper level ridge shifts east over our \narea Thursday and Friday. On Thursday, expect mostly clear skies, \nwith temperatures reaching mid-to-high 60s. However, Friday should \nbe slightly \"cooler\" (low/mid 60s) as cloud cover will increase \nahead of an incoming system.  \n\nAn upper level shortwave will track across Colorado on Saturday \nbefore becoming a closed low over the Oklahoma/Texas panhandle by \nSunday morning. This system will bring weak QG forcing and \nlimited moisture into the region, mainly allowing mountain snow to\nfall Saturday and Sunday. Exactly how much snowfall is dependent \non the strength of the system and moisture availability, but model\ntrends have been consistent with light snow accumulations over \nthe weekend. This could lead to minor travel impacts to high \nmountain passes as roads could become slick late Saturday and into\nSunday morning. As for the plains, model guidance has continued \nto slowly back off on precipitation given the more southerly track\nof the system and overall lack of moisture transport into the \nregion. This is congruent with the recent regional model outputs, \nwith Lincoln county having the best chance for measurable \nprecipitation Saturday night into Sunday. Portions of the eastern\nplains and Palmer Divide may still see a few light rain showers,\nbut chances are pretty low (10-20%). \n\nThis weekend will also see a nice cooldown compared to this past \nweek, with more seasonable temperatures arriving by Saturday. Expect \nhighs to be in the mid 50s across the plains both Saturday and \nSunday. \n\nMonday and most of Tuesday should see a return of quiet weather as a \nweak upper level ridge shifts into Colorado. This will allow for a \nbrief warm-up, but still expect temperatures in the high 50s, \npossibly reaching 60 F in the Denver metro area. \n\nOur next system is progged to arrive sometime Wednesday. However, it \nis too soon to tell how exactly this disturbance will impact our \nforecast area, as model guidance varies greatly this far out. As of \nright now though, the mountains have a better chance receiving \nrain/snow showers out of this system than the plains. Will be \nkeeping an eye on it. Looking farther out, ensembles favor the \nactive weather pattern continuing.",
